   "source": [
    "# DFT Study of Metal-Enabled SNAR Activity\n",
    "## Tau Arylation PTM and analysis\n",
    "**Kylie Short | Computational Physical Organic Chemistry**\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Biological Motivation: Alzheimer's Disease & the Tau Protein\n",
    "\n",
    "Alzheimer's disease (AD) is the leading cause of dementia worldwide, affecting over **55 million people** and\n",
    "representing the third leading cause of death in developed nations. Despite decades of research, no\n",
    "disease-modifying cure exists. Current treatments only manage symptoms.[1]\n",
    "\n",
    "**Tau** is a microtubule-associated protein (MAP) expressed predominantly in neurons. Its primary role is to bind and stabilize microtubules. \n",
    "\n",
    "In Alzheimer's disease, post translational modification ( PTMs) occur on Tau. This reduces its affinity for microtubules, causing detachment, misfolding, and aggregation into paired helical filaments (PHFs) that form **neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs)**.\n",
    "The progression of NFTs through the brain correlates closely with cognitive decline in AD.[1]\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "82a4e456",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"750\"/>\n",
    "\n",
    "**Figure:** Healthy vs. diseased neuron. In healthy neurons Tau stabilises microtubules (top). In Alzheimer's disease, Tau loses microtubule association, misfolds, and forms neurofibrillary tangles (bottom). (From: Role of Tau in Various Tauopathies, Treatment Approaches, and Emerging Role of Nanotechnology in Neurodegenerative Disorders)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "4e204c84-ec25-48f6-85b6-25d0ec315b13.png": {
     "image/png": ""
    },
    "9b8fca9a-9b24-4b42-9509-38e09e89d57b.png": {
     "image/png": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "90b984ca",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Why Tau is Hard to Study\n",
    "\n",
    "Tau is an **intrinsically disordered protein (IDP)** — unlike folded enzymes or receptors, it lacks\n",
    "a stable three-dimensional structure and has no well-defined hydrophobic binding pocket. This makes studying it so hard because PTMs occur often occur nonselectively.\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"attachment:9b8fca9a-9b24-4b42-9509-38e09e89d57b.png\" width=\"680\" style=\"display:block; margin:12px 0;\"/>\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "A covelant approach to arylation could offer a route around this problem. Tau has only two cysteine residues. Because cysteines are rare in Tau and have a reactive thiol (–SH) side chain, they are ideal\n",
    "targets for selective electrophilic arylation via SNAr.[2] \n",
    "\n",
    "### Post-Translational Modifications (PTMs) of Tau - arylation\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"attachment:4e204c84-ec25-48f6-85b6-25d0ec315b13.png\" width=\"620\" style=\"display:block; margin:12px 0;\"/>\n",
    "\n",
    "*Figure: Arylation PTM — covalent attachment of an aryl group to the Tau cysteine thiol via\n",
    "nucleophilic aromatic substitution, enabled by metal-activated ring electrophiles.*[3]\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "25827236-6149-465e-992e-0f51ea60a54a.png": {
     "image/png": ""
    },
    "6b7299fe-7a7b-43a7-b6ef-b7b75584f343.png": {
     "image/png": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ad002327",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## SNAR Mechanism: Why Benzene Fails — and How Fe/Ru/Mn Complexes Succeed\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"attachment:6b7299fe-7a7b-43a7-b6ef-b7b75584f343.png\" width=\"680\" style=\"display:block; margin:12px 0;\"/>\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why benzilic rings fail:**\n",
    "\n",
    "Biphenyl, naphthalene, anthracene, and tetracene are electron-neutral to slightly electron-donating.\n",
    "- Their LUMO energies are too high (~1.5–3.0 eV)[4]\n",
    "-  the intermediate is too destabilised\n",
    "-  tempratures are not biologically compatable with protiens\n",
    "\n",
    "### How η⁶-Metal Coordination Solves Both Problems\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"attachment:25827236-6149-465e-992e-0f51ea60a54a.png\" width=\"680\" style=\"display:block; margin:12px 0;\"/>\n",
    "\n",
    "*Figure: η⁶-metal coordination to the arene ring face. The metal sits directly above the ring,\n",
    "interacting with all six π-electrons.*\n",
    "\n",
    "When a metal center (Fe²⁺, Ru²⁺, or Mn⁺) coordinates to the ring face** via η⁶ hapticity,\n",
    "it acts as a powerful π-acid:\n",
    "- lowers the energy of the transition state by being electron withdrawing\n",
    "- biologically compatable conditions\n",
    "- can have much farther distances from the metal to the leaving group allowing for a much broader use\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "id": "e303d985",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      " All libraries loaded\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import matplotlib.patches as mpatches\n",
    "from sklearn.cluster import AgglomerativeClustering, KMeans\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "from sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n",
    "from scipy.cluster.hierarchy import dendrogram, linkage\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Notebook display settings\n",
    "pd.set_option('display.max_rows', 120)\n",
    "pd.set_option('display.float_format', '{:.3f}'.format)\n",
    "plt.rcParams.update({\n",
    "    'figure.dpi': 120,\n",
    "    'axes.spines.top': False,\n",
    "    'axes.spines.right': False,\n",
    "    'font.family': 'sans-serif',\n",
    "})\n",
    "print(\" All libraries loaded\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "34392660-2f79-4642-bfeb-e3488a48ba97.png": {
     "image/png": ""
    },
    "a6c24f69-a976-4d04-8b72-df7a93b0325c.png": {
     "image/png": ""
